{{ project_description }}

Generated from the
[uv-devcontainer](https://github.com/ShaneMcGovern/copier-uv-devcontainer) Copier
template. The template supplies the parts that are tedious to assemble and
easy to get subtly wrong: a reproducible dev container, a locked `uv`
environment, and a pre-commit gate that runs the same way on your machine and
in CI{% if use_semantic_release %}, plus releases
driven by commit messages{% endif %}. `.copier-answers.yml` records your
answers. That lets `uvx copier update --trust` pull in later template
improvements without losing your changes.

## Features

- **Reproducible dependencies** with
  [uv](https://github.com/astral-sh/uv) - a committed lockfile and no
  virtualenv to activate
- **Pre-configured tooling**: pytest with coverage, ruff linting and formatting,
  mypy in strict mode
- **One quality gate** shared by your machine and CI, so nothing passes locally
  and fails on push
{% if use_semantic_release -%}
- **GitHub Actions CI/CD** with automated testing and releases driven by commit
  messages
{% else -%}
- **GitHub Actions CI** with automated testing and coverage on every push
{% endif -%}
- **Zero host setup** - Docker Compose dev container, non-root user, persistent
  caches

## Installation

**Prerequisites**: [Docker](https://docs.docker.com/get-docker/),
[VS Code](https://code.visualstudio.com/), and the
[Dev Containers extension](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=ms-vscode-remote.remote-containers).
No Python and no uv on the host.

First, check your git identity. `.devcontainer/.env` was created when this
project was generated, from the author details you gave then:

```bash
GIT_AUTHOR_NAME="{{ author_name }}"
GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL="{{ author_email }}"
```

Edit it if that isn't who should be committing here. The file is gitignored, so
it stays local to your checkout. `.devcontainer/.env.example` is the committed
copy and carries placeholders rather than these values. Anyone else who clones
this repository copies it and fills in their own identity.

The container receives these values, and `entrypoint.sh` applies them, so
commits made inside the container carry the right author. They must not be
empty. Git reads them directly, and they outrank `user.name`, so a blank value
fails every commit with `fatal: empty ident name`.

### Option 1: VS Code Dev Container (Recommended)

Open the folder in VS Code, then click **"Reopen in Container"** or use the
Command Palette (`Cmd/Ctrl+Shift+P`) and run **"Dev Containers: Reopen in Container"**.

On first start the container syncs the environment (`uv sync`) and installs the
pre-commit hooks. Both are automatic.

### Option 2: Docker Compose

Run the container directly with Docker Compose:

```bash
# Build and start the container
docker compose -f .devcontainer/compose.yaml up -d --build

# Open a shell inside it
docker compose -f .devcontainer/compose.yaml exec app bash

# Stop the container
docker compose -f .devcontainer/compose.yaml down
```

`entrypoint.sh` applies your git identity, runs `uv sync`, and installs the
pre-commit hooks on every start. `devcontainer.json` is a thin wrapper over this
same compose file. It adds editor settings and nothing else, so the two entry
points can't drift apart.

**Start the container before your first push.** A freshly generated project has
no `uv.lock`: that first `uv sync` writes it, using the interpreter already
inside the image. Generation skips building it on purpose, so it cannot depend
on what Python your *host* happens to have. Both workflows install with
`uv sync --frozen`, so CI needs the lock committed. Start the container, let it
sync, and commit the `uv.lock` it produces along with everything else.

Either way, **commit from inside the container**. The git hooks call `uv run`,
so they need the container's toolchain. A `git commit` from the host will try
to use a host Python and fail. The VS Code terminal already is a container
shell.

## Quick Start

Once the container is running:

```bash
# Run the tests, with coverage
uv run pytest

# Run the application
uv run python -m main
# Output: Hello from {{ project_slug }}!

# Run every hook, exactly as CI runs them
uv run pre-commit run --all-files
```

`python -m main` works inside the container because `compose.yaml` sets
`PYTHONPATH=/app/src`. Outside it, pass the path yourself:
`PYTHONPATH=src uv run python -m main`. This project isn't a package, so `src/`
is not installed and nothing puts it on `sys.path` for you.

[CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) has the everyday commands for changing the
project: linting, type checking, adding a dependency, and the full hook list.

{% if use_semantic_release %}## CI and Releases

Two workflows, both in `.github/workflows/`.
{% else %}## Continuous Integration

One workflow, in `.github/workflows/`.
{% endif %}
**`validate.yml`** runs on every branch push and on PRs to `main`:

1. `pre-commit` - every hook, across all files.
2. `test` - pytest, uploading `coverage.xml` as an artifact.
3. `badge` - on `main` only, regenerates `badges/coverage.svg` from that
   artifact and commits it if it changed.
{% if use_semantic_release %}
**`release.yml`** runs on pushes to `main` and hands off to
python-semantic-release, which reads its configuration from `pyproject.toml`.
Releases come from commit messages: the type prefix decides what happens.
[CONTRIBUTING.md](CONTRIBUTING.md) has the vocabulary and says which prefixes
ship a release.

On a release, python-semantic-release bumps the version in `pyproject.toml`,
the only place it appears. It inserts the new entry into `CHANGELOG.md` below
its `<!-- version list -->` marker and re-locks `uv.lock` so its recorded
version follows. Then it tags and publishes a GitHub Release.

Nothing installs this repo, so no distribution artifacts are built or
published. `build_command` runs `uv lock` instead. That keeps `uv.lock`'s
recorded project version in step with the bump PSR just wrote into
`pyproject.toml`, and `assets` carries the regenerated lock into the release
commit. Without it, a release would leave the lock stale and fail the `uv-lock`
hook on the next push.

The `badge` job and `release.yml` share a `concurrency` group, so they can't
race each other pushing to `main`.
{% endif %}
## Updating from the Template

This project was generated from a Copier template, and `.copier-answers.yml`
records which version and which answers. To pull in later template improvements:

```bash
# Re-ask each question, prior answer as the default
uvx copier update --trust

# Reuse every prior answer, no prompts
uvx copier update --trust --defaults
```

**Commit your work first**: Copier refuses to update a dirty tree. It then
re-applies the template and merges the result with your changes, prompting where
they conflict.

`update` moves to the template's newest **tag**, so an untagged template change
is invisible to it. And `uvx` fetches whatever Copier version is current, so
pin it (`uvx copier@9.17.2`) when the update needs to be reproducible.

Some template releases may restructure a generated project in ways `update`
cannot merge on its own. Jumping across one leaves you to reconcile the result
by hand. When that happens, the template's
[UPGRADING.md](https://github.com/ShaneMcGovern/copier-uv-devcontainer/blob/main/docs/UPGRADING.md)
records what changed and the order to step through. Read it before updating
across several versions at once.

